214:	Both laws are push-forwards of the uniform distribution on $V$ along a coordinate of the CL map of Definition~\ref{def:cl-dist}, so in each case the event may be pulled back to the underlying sample $z=(u,s,v)$. For the axis law the recorded direction is the coordinate vector $e_{\chi(s)}$, which is nonzero, so the pulled-back event is empty. For the diagonal law, Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} gives the recorded direction $v' = \pi_{i-1}(v)$ with $i = \chi(s)$, and $\pi_{i-1}$ is idempotent by Lemma~\ref{lem:prefix-projection-idempotent}. Since $i \le m$, the prefix projection leaves the final direction coordinate unchanged. Thus $v'=0$ forces that sampled coordinate to vanish. It is uniform on $\F_q$, being a marginal of the uniform distribution on $V$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:uniform-map-equivalence} and Lemma~\ref{lem:uniform-product-first-marginal}, composed by Lemma~\ref{lem:distribution-map-comp}), and each field element has weight $1/q$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:uniform-distribution-weight}. Monotonicity of the average over the pointwise inequality of indicators gives the bound.
